\subsection{Repo-Level Results}
\label{sec:repo}

Table~\ref{tab:repo_level} compares \cls{} and \cwm{} on SWE-bench Verified per-test data (Qwen3-4B, LoRA, seed 42, max sequence length 4,096).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Repo-level per-test verification on SWE-bench Verified (Qwen3-4B, seed 42). \cwm{} falls below majority baseline, indicating negative utility. Unparseable: fraction of outputs that cannot be parsed into a verdict.}
\label{tab:repo_level}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Acc.\%} & \textbf{Fail \fone{}} & \textbf{Pass \fone{}} & \textbf{Macro \fone{}} & \textbf{Unparse.\%} \\
\midrule
\cls{} & \textbf{76.92} & \textbf{65.01} & \textbf{82.78} & \textbf{73.90} & 1.3 \\
\cwm{} & 66.60 & 27.70 & 78.30 & 52.99 & 75.2 \\
Majority & 72.96 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

At repository-level complexity, the classification advantage transitions from modest to critical. \cls{} achieves 76.92\% accuracy with macro \fone{} 73.90\%, while \cwm{} falls to 66.60\%---below the majority baseline (72.96\%) by 6.36\pp{}, indicating negative utility. The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens from 1.24\pp{} at function level to 10.32\pp{} at repo level, an 8.3$\times$ amplification.

The primary driver is token-budget exhaustion: 75.2\% of \cwm{} outputs are unparseable, compared to 1.3\% for \cls{}. At repo-level, the mean expected output length is 3,398 tokens, far exceeding the 1,024-token generation budget---structurally guaranteeing failure for the majority of test cases. \cls{}, producing a single binary token per test, is immune to this failure mode by construction. These results provide direct evidence for the complexity-dependent advantage hypothesized in \S\ref{sec:failure-modes}: \cwm{} failure modes that are latent at function level become dominant at repo-level complexity.

We note that these results are from a single seed; additional seeds are in progress. The magnitude of the gap (10.32\pp{}) and the structural nature of token-budget exhaustion suggest the finding is robust, but seed-level replication is needed to confirm.

\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

