We note that the complexity-dependent advantage remains a hypothesis supported by scaling evidence; direct validation on repository-level code is in progress.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
The function-level near-equivalence (1.24\pp{}) limits practical \cls{} advantage at that scale. All experiments use a single model family (Qwen3); scaling behavior may differ across architectures. The oracle analysis assumes ground-truth test labels; real-world advantage is upper-bounded by classifier accuracy. P2P regression rates (${\sim}$1\% mean) are too low for per-test P2P signals to be useful---diagnostic advantage is limited to the F2P dimension. Error complementarity (oracle 90.63\%) has not yet been exploited in a working ensemble.

\paragraph{Future work.}
\cls{}--\cwm{} error complementarity motivates ensemble verification routing by predicted output complexity. Per-test failure signals enable diagnostic re-repair: localizing faults from per-test verdicts rather than discarding failed patches. Repo-level evaluation is in progress. Multi-model validation and integration with test-time scaling systems (best-of-$N$ with per-test reranking) are natural next steps.

